  "rationale": "Only non-perfect judge is summary_quality=0.934 (headroom 0.033). Round 4 addressed conciseness (+0.0016 kept). Remaining headroom lives in the other three sub-criteria (accuracy, completeness, faithfulness). The one failure mode no active skill speaks to is faithfulness-by-invention: the current bullets instruct what to INCLUDE (core requirement, verbatim terms, precise language) but never what to EXCLUDE content-wise, and a legal summarizer under 'preserve defined terms + cross-references' pressure often adds a short interpretive gloss (e.g. 'This means that...' or an illustrative example not in the source) that the judge docks faithfulness 5\u21924 on. I add exactly ONE bullet forbidding additions. It is strictly length-REDUCING \u2014 the mechanical opposite of Round 1's catastrophic length-inflating cascade (\u22120.106), which combined coverage-push + no-paraphrase + normative-obligation and 32k-truncated two examples to 0/0/0. Removing the coverage-push component (which was the inflation driver) and keeping only the no-add constraint cannot reproduce that failure. Materially different from Round 4 (brevity parenthetical, sentence count) \u2014 this bullet is about content SOURCE (don't invent), not length. No clash: identity.md says 'faithful short summary' (aligned); hierarchy/citations/exclusions/output_schema are structural (orthogonal); no active rule speaks to summary content. Predicted \u0394: +0.003 to +0.010 on summary_quality via faithfulness-criterion flips on 2\u20134 examples currently at 0.9375. Downside: true noop if the model already avoids invention at T=0.1, or at worst a tiny completeness nudge of ~0.003 (strictly bounded because the constraint only forbids additions, it does not remove anything the previous bullets required).",
